Raw states for a rewind

A rewind takes a state every few seconds and keeps it in memory, never on
disk. retro_serialize compresses every state with zstd, and for a New 3DS
game's 296 MB state that compression is most of its cost (Tools/3ds-bench
rewind, Majora's Mask 3D: 590 to 860 ms a state on the host).

This adds System::SaveStateRaw / LoadStateRaw, the state as the archive writes
it, with no header and no compression, written into the caller's buffer (its
capacity kept between calls, so the 296 MB is allocated once), and two entry
points for a frontend that knows them, retro_ctr_rewind_capture and
retro_ctr_rewind_restore, which drain the pending async operations first as
retro_serialize and retro_unserialize do. Not a format to store: valid only for
the build and title that wrote it.

